param {
group.0 {
name=Dynamically Reconfigurable Parameters
desc=See the [[dynamic_reconfigure]] package for details on dynamically reconfigurable parameters.
0.name= ~running
0.default= True
0.type= bool
0.desc=Pause/Resume simulation 
1.name= ~admin_hash
1.default= 
1.type= str
1.desc=Admin hash for evaluation mode 
2.name= ~integrator
2.default= 0
2.type= int
2.desc=Numerical integrator for forward dynamics in continuous time. Possible values are: Implicitfast (3): Implicit-in-velocity Euler with Cholesky. The implicitfast integrator has similar computational cost to Euler, yet provides increased stability, and is therefore a strict improvement. It is the recommended integrator for most models., Implicit (2): Implicit-in-velocity Euler. The benefit over implicitfast is the implicit integration of Coriolis and centripetal forces, including gyroscopic forces. The most common case where integrating such forces implicitly leads to noticable improvement is when free objects with assymetric inertia are spinning quickly., RK4 (1): Runge-Kutta 4th order multi-step. This integrator is best for systems which are energy conserving, or almost energy-conserving., Euler (0): Semi-implicit Euler with joint damping. Use Euler for compatibillity with older models.
3.name= ~cone
3.default= 1
3.type= int
3.desc=Type of contact friction cone. Possible values are: Elliptic (1): Model friction as a smooth, continuous force constraint within an elliptical base within physically realistic bounds., Pyramidal (0): Approximate the friction constraint using a pyramidal base, which simplifies computation but may introduce discontinuities or inaccuracies
4.name= ~jacobian
4.default= 2
4.type= int
4.desc=Type Jacobian and matrices computed from it. Possible values are: Dense (0): Dense Jacobian., Sparse (1): Sparse Jacobian, Auto (2): Dense up to 60 DoF, and sparse over 60 DoF
5.name= ~solver
5.default= 2
5.type= int
5.desc=Type of solver to use for constraint forces. Possible values are: Newton (2): This algorithm implements the exact Newton method, with analytical second-order derivatives and Cholesky factorization of the Hessian. The line-search is the same as in the CG method. It is the default solver., PGS (0): PGS uses the dual formulation. Unlike gradient-based methods which improve the solution along oblique directions, Gauss-Seidel works on one scalar component at a time, and sets it to its optimal value given the current values of all other components. One sweep of PGS has the computational complexity of one matrix-vector multiplication (although the constants are larger). It has first-order convergence but nevertheless makes rapid progress in a few iterations., CG (1): This algorithm uses the non-linear conjugate gradient method with the Polak-Ribiere-Plus formula. Line-search is exact, using Newton’s method in one dimension, with analytical second derivatives.
6.name= ~timestep
6.default= 0.001
6.type= double
6.desc=Simulation step in seconds Range: 1e-10 to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
7.name= ~iterations
7.default= 100
7.type= int
7.desc=Maximum number of iterations of the constraint solver. When the warmstart attribute of flag is enabled (which is the default), accurate results are obtained with fewer iterations. Larger and more complex systems with many interacting constraints require more iterations. Note that mjData.solver contains statistics about solver convergence, also shown in the profiler. Range: 1 to 2147483647
8.name= ~tolerance
8.default= 1e-08
8.type= double
8.desc=Tolerance threshold used for early termination of the iterative solver. For PGS, the threshold is applied to the cost improvement between two iterations. For CG and Newton, it is applied to the smaller of the cost improvement and the gradient norm. Set the tolerance to 0 to disable early termination. Range: 0.0 to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
9.name= ~ls_iter
9.default= 50
9.type= int
9.desc=Maximum number of linesearch iterations performed by CG/Newton constraint solvers. Ensures that at most iterations times ls_iterations linesearch iterations are performed during each constraint solve. Range: 1 to 2147483647
10.name= ~ls_tol
10.default= 0.01
10.type= double
10.desc=Tolerance threshold used for early termination of the linesearch algorithm. Range: 0.0 to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
11.name= ~noslip_iter
11.default= 0
11.type= int
11.desc=Maximum number of iterations of the Noslip solver. This is a post-processing step executed after the main solver. It uses a modified PGS method to suppress slip/drift in friction dimensions resulting from the soft-constraint model. The default setting 0 disables this post-processing step. Range: 0 to 2147483647
12.name= ~noslip_tol
12.default= 1e-06
12.type= double
12.desc=Tolerance threshold used for early termination of the Noslip solver. Range: 0.0 to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
13.name= ~ccd_iter
13.default= 50
13.type= int
13.desc=Maximum number of iterations of the algorithm used for convex collisions (CCD). This rarely needs to be adjusted, except in situations where some geoms have very large aspect ratios. Range: 1 to 2147483647
14.name= ~ccd_tol
14.default= 1e-06
14.type= double
14.desc=Tolerance threshold used for early termination of the convex collision algorithm. Range: 0.0 to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
15.name= ~sdf_iter
15.default= 10
15.type= int
15.desc=Number of iterations used for Signed Distance Field collisions (per initial point). Range: 1 to 2147483647
16.name= ~sdf_init
16.default= 40
16.type= int
16.desc=Number of starting points used for finding contacts with Signed Distance Field collisions. Range: 1 to 2147483647
17.name= ~gravity
17.default= 0 0 -9.81
17.type= str
17.desc=Gravitational acceleration vector. In the default world orientation the Z-axis points up. 
18.name= ~wind
18.default= 0 0 0
18.type= str
18.desc=Velocity vector of the medium (i.e., wind). This vector is subtracted from the 3D translational velocity of each body, and the result is used to compute viscous, lift and drag forces acting on the body. 
19.name= ~magnetic
19.default= 0 -0.5 0
19.type= str
19.desc=Global magnetic flux. This vector is used by magnetometer sensors, which return the magnetic flux at their pose expressed in their frame. 
20.name= ~density
20.default= 0.0
20.type= double
20.desc=Density of the medium, not to be confused with the geom density used to infer masses and inertias. This parameter is used to simulate lift and drag forces, which scale quadratically with velocity. In SI units the density of air is around 1.2 while the density of water is around 1000 depending on temperature. Setting  density to 0 disables lift and drag forces. Range: -std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity() to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
21.name= ~viscosity
21.default= 0.0
21.type= double
21.desc=Viscosity of the medium. This parameter is used to simulate viscous forces, which scale linearly with velocity. In SI units the visocsity of air is around 0.00002 while the viscosity of water is around 0.00009 depending on temperature. Setting viscosity to 0 disables viscous forces. Note that the default Euler integrator handles damping in the joints implicitly - which improves stability and accuracy. It does not presently do this with body viscosity. Therefore, if the goal is merely to create a damped simulation (as opposed to modeling the specific effects of viscosity), we revommend using joint damping rather thad body viscosity, or switching to the implicit or implicitfast integrators. Range: -std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity() to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
22.name= ~impratio
22.default= 1.0
22.type= double
22.desc=This attribute determines the ratio of frictional-to-normal constraint impedance for elliptic  Range: -std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity() to std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity()
